# =============================================================================
# HireSnap - build context filter for the SUPERPROJECT ROOT.
#
# WHY this file exists at the root and not under backend/:
# the backend image is built with "context: .." (docker/docker-compose.yaml:70-73,
# mirroring R:\GPTHub\docker\docker-compose.yaml:32-37), so Docker reads
# <context>/.dockerignore - this file - and never backend/.dockerignore. It is the
# only thing standing between a "docker build" and a multi-gigabyte context made
# of the MySQL data directory, uploaded videos, model weights and node_modules.
# Mirrors R:\GPTHub\.dockerignore:1-14.
#
# A large context is not merely slow: BuildKit ships every file it does not
# ignore to the daemon BEFORE reading the Dockerfile, so an ignored-after-the-fact
# 20 GB data directory turns a 30-second build into a ten-minute one and, on a
# remote daemon, into a failed upload.
# =============================================================================

# --- secrets ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Real values for dev and prod live beside the compose files. Only the template
# is allowed through, because the frontend build reads REACT_APP_* from it and a
# missing manifest makes the bundle fall back to a hardcoded URL.
# Mirror of R:\GPTHub\.dockerignore:3-5.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# --- VCS and per-service dependency trees ----------------------------------
# .git is the single biggest thing that can leak into a context: submodules keep
# their own HEADs and object stores under .git/modules.
.git
**/.git
**/.venv
**/venv
**/node_modules

# --- live stack state (bind mounts) ---------------------------------------
# Uploaded videos and .npy embeddings, the MySQL data directory and TLS/JWT
# material are HOST state. Copying them into the image would ship one
# deployment's user data inside a distributable artifact.
# infra/storage and infra/mysql_data are also .gitignore'd (.gitignore:43-44);
# the duplication is deliberate - .gitignore protects the repository, this file
# protects the image, and the two lists are maintained for different reasons.
infra/storage
infra/mysql_data
infra/logs
infra/models
infra/nginx/certs
infra/certs
docker/.run-state

# --- model weights ---------------------------------------------------------
# Multi-GB, licence-bound, mounted read-only into the ml sidecar at run time.
# Never part of a build.
#
# NOTE: the sidecar builds with "context: ../ml", so it does NOT read this file -
# it reads ml/.dockerignore. The two files are not duplicates: this one protects
# the API image (whose context is the superproject root), that one protects the
# sidecar image (whose context is the ml submodule). A weight directory under
# backend/ or at the root is still the root file's job, which is why these
# patterns stay here as well.
**/src/weights
**/pretrained_ckpts
**/weights
**/*.pt
**/*.pth
**/*.onnx
**/*.bin
**/*.npy
**/*.h5
**/*.ckpt

# --- documentation and tooling that the image never imports ---------------
# docs/ is prose. scripts/ are guards that run on the host and in CI. Neither is
# imported at run time, and both change on almost every commit, so keeping them
# in the context would invalidate the COPY layer for no benefit.
docs
scripts
.github
**/*.md

# --- build output and test caches -----------------------------------------
# backend/build/ and frontend/build/ are CRA/packaging output; the images build
# their own. Caches below are per-machine and only add churn to the cache key.
**/build
**/dist
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/coverage
**/.coverage
**/htmlcov

# --- editor and OS noise --------------------------------------------------
.idea
.vscode
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.log
*.tmp

# --- snapshot files that WOULD otherwise be useful ------------------------
# The backend image needs backend/requirements.txt, requirements.lock.txt,
# backend/env.py, backend/create_sql.py, backend/HireSnap.sql and backend/src.
# Nothing above excludes those, which is why the ignore list stays narrow instead
# of using the tempting "backend/** then un-ignore" pattern: un-ignoring inside an
# ignored subtree cannot resurrect a file whose parent directory was pruned.
#
# The ML stack is deliberately absent from this list: it does not belong in the
# API image any more (scripts/check_ml_boundary.py is the gate that enforces it),
# and ml/ is built from its own context rather than copied through this one.
